From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 16:00:00 +0800
Subject: phy: cix: fix silent probe failures, clock bug, and ACPI reset-lookup race

The CIX USB2/USB3/USB-DP PHY drivers and the ACPI resource-lookup
helper had several bugs that can leave the USB-DP/USB3 PHY
mis-programmed or held out of reset without clocks. These bugs can
leave the PHY incompletely initialized or in an undefined reset/clock
state and may contribute to the SuperSpeed link resets and storage
failures observed on usb14 under sustained I/O on the Minisforum MS-R1
(CIX Sky1); that causal link is not proven by this patch alone.

 * phy-cix-usb3.c: cix_u3phy_probe() checked IS_ERR(u3phy->apb_clk)
   twice - once for apb_clk and again (copy/paste) instead of
   checking apb_clk for the ref_clk lookup, so a missing ref_clk was
   never detected. This is confirmed to be an unreviewed copy/paste
   mistake rather than an intentional hardware/firmware workaround:
   the same double-check-of-apb_clk pattern is present, unchanged and
   without any explanatory comment, in both the original CIX vendor
   patch series and Sky1-Linux's own current upstream-targeted tree,
   and there is no clk-framework mechanism by which fetching ref_clk
   could plausibly alter apb_clk's error state. A code comment noting
   this (and that the vendor has it the other way around) is added at
   the fixed call site in phy-cix-usb3.c.

 * phy-cix-usb2.c, phy-cix-usb3.c, phy-cix-usbdp.c: required
   reset/preset controls and clocks were fetched with
   devm_reset_control_get()/devm_clk_get() and, on failure, only
   dev_err()-logged before probe continued. Since these drivers
   unconditionally call reset/clock APIs on the stored pointer later,
   a missing lookup previously meant an ERR_PTR could be retained and
   dereferenced. These now return dev_err_probe() with the real errno
   and abort probe.

 * phy-cix-usbdp.c: sky1_udphy_init() returned 0 (success) on every
   error path even when `ret` held a real error from clock
   enablement, reset deassertion, or register programming, hiding PHY
   init failures from callers who then believed the PHY was ready. It
   now returns the real error.

 * phy-cix-usb3.c, phy-cix-usbdp.c: reset_control_deassert() return
   values were not checked in the init paths, so a failed deassert
   (PHY left held in reset) was not distinguished from success. These
   are now checked and cause an error return.

 * phy-cix-usb3.c, phy-cix-usbdp.c: the error-cleanup labels reached
   on a later failure asserted reset/preset, but were actually calling
   reset_control_deassert() - i.e. releasing the PHY out of reset with
   clocks off and registers unprogrammed, rather than the safe
   held-in-reset idle state the labels implied. Fixed to actually
   assert.

 * drivers/soc/cix/cix-acpi-resource-lookup.c: the CIXA1019 driver
   injects _DSD reset/clock/resource-name properties into consumer
   ACPI devices (CIXH2030-2034) at probe time, but had no way for
   those consumers to tell "property not injected yet" apart from
   "property genuinely absent". A consumer that raced ahead of
   injection previously got a hard lookup failure rather than a
   deferral. This adds an exported readiness signal
   (cix_acpi_resource_lookup_ready()) and a
   cix_acpi_resource_lookup_defer() helper (new header
   include/linux/soc/cix/cix-acpi-resource-lookup.h) that consumers
   use to convert a reset/clock lookup failure into -EPROBE_DEFER
   while injection is pending, and let the real error through once
   injection is known complete.

Also adds concise dev_info() probe-summary diagnostics (PHY id, mode,
default_conf, phy-status, clock rate, reset/preset acquisition) for
the usb2/usb3/usbdp PHYs, populates the previously-unused
udphy->phy_status field from the ACPI "phy-status" property so that
diagnostic is accurate, and adds dev_dbg()/dev_warn_ratelimited()
around PHY init/exit transitions and orientation/mode-mux changes
while the PHY is configured for USB (the driver does not track host
traffic or link activity, only PHY lane-mode configuration, so the
diagnostics are worded accordingly).

This is the first of three related patches: a second patch hardens
reset/preset assert() sequencing and cleanup-path correctness in
phy-cix-usb3.c/phy-cix-usbdp.c, and a third narrows and hardens the
ACPI readiness mechanism added here (error-code scoping, memory
ordering, lifecycle documentation).

Signed-off-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
Change-Id: I0072000000000000000000000000000000000072
